        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "3af5050b-3524-52c3-acc7-ec48b2c27123",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Alexander Glazunov, Marius Petipa, Jānis Liepiņš\n- Works: Symphony No. 9 in D minor, Raymonda, Die Fledermaus, Gianni Schicchi, Eugene Onegin, Romeo and Juliet, Scheherezade and Her Tales, La Bayadere\n- Organizations: Saint Petersburg Conservatory, Petrograd Conservatory, Leningrad Conservatory\n- Roles: director, conductor, musical director, choreographer, composer\n- Events: Bolshevik Revolution\n\nFacts:\n- Alexander Glazunov served as director of the Saint Petersburg Conservatory from 1905 to 1928.\n- Following the Bolshevik Revolution, Alexander Glazunov helped reorganize the Saint Petersburg Conservatory into the Petrograd Conservatory and later the Leningrad Conservatory.\n- Alexander Glazunov began composing Symphony No. 9 in D minor in 1910.\n- Alexander Glazunov's Symphony No. 9 in D minor was still unfinished when he died in 1936.\n- Raymonda is a ballet in three acts and four scenes with an apotheosis, choreographed by Marius Petipa with music by Alexander Glazunov as his Opus 57.\n- Jānis Liepiņš was the conductor and musical director for the ballet Raymonda and the operetta Die Fledermaus.\n- Jānis Liepiņš also conducted Gianni Schicchi, Eugene Onegin, Romeo and Juliet, Scheherezade and Her Tales, and La Bayadere.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
